// What generated code calls is not what a program may name.
//
// An entry point answering `Result` is unwrapped where it returns, and the
// failing arm calls the library's `report`. A test binary's entry point is
// written by the compiler too, and calls `selected`, `ran` and `no_such_test`.
// Lowering finds each by its module and its name -- `core::report`,
// `testing::selected` -- rather than through what the prelude publishes, so
// they are reachable by the compiler and by nothing else.
//
// Both ways of asking are refused, and they are refused for different reasons.
// Unqualified, the name is simply not in scope: the prelude publishes what the
// language's own syntax obliges a program to write, and nothing a programmer
// types names this. Qualified, the module holding it is imported and the name
// is found and then refused -- it is private, and visibility is what decides
// whether a program may write a name it can reach.
//
// The second is the one worth writing down. A library module is importable, so
// what keeps this out of a program's reach is that it is private. Publishing one
// to let the compiler reach it is what that refuses, since the compiler needs no
// such help: a generated call names a module and a name, and visibility never
// enters into finding one.
// All four, because the one left out is the one somebody publishes.
import std.testing
import std.io
import std.io.core

fn main() -> UInt8 {
    let _unqualified = report(1)
    let _ran = core.ran(io.println("a"))
    let _selected = testing.selected("a")
    let _no_such = testing.no_such_test()
    core.report(1)
}
